consiSt("ri",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Rhode Island Supreme Court","d":"The state’s only appellate court; there is no intermediate court of appeals. Hears appeals from the Superior and Family Courts, petitions for certiorari and other extraordinary writs, and certified questions from federal courts and Rhode Island trial courts. Also controls bar admission, pro hac vice policy, lawyer discipline, and the statewide e-filing rules (Article X)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURT (GENERAL)","n":"Superior Court","d":"General-jurisdiction trial court sitting in four venues (Providence/Bristol, Kent, Newport, Washington). Exclusive original jurisdiction of actions at law over $10,000, concurrent with the District Court from $5,000 to $10,000, plus equity, title to real estate, and review of many agency decisions. Has a business calendar and mandatory court-annexed arbitration for money claims of $100,000 or less. Hears District Court civil appeals de novo."},{"l":"TRIAL COURT (LIMITED)","n":"District Court","d":"Exclusive original jurisdiction of civil actions at law up to $5,000, all landlord-tenant possession actions, small replevin, housing-code and ordinance cases; concurrent jurisdiction with the Superior Court from $5,000 to $10,000 (a defendant may demand removal to Superior Court in the answer). A losing party may claim a de novo appeal to the Superior Court within 2 business days after judgment."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED COURTS","n":"Family Court, Workers’ Compensation Court, and Rhode Island Traffic Tribunal","d":"Statewide courts with subject-matter jurisdiction set by statute. Family Court appeals go directly to the Supreme Court under Article I. Each of these courts may admit out-of-state lawyers pro hac vice under the delegation in Sup. Ct. Art. II, Rule 9(a)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: R.I. Gen. Laws ch. 9-33 (conditional immunity, not a dismissal framework)","d":"Rhode Island’s 1993 anti-SLAPP law makes a party’s exercise of the right of petition or free speech, under the U.S. or Rhode Island constitutions, in connection with a matter of public concern, conditionally immune from civil claims, counterclaims, and cross-claims directed at that activity.\nCovered activity is defined as any written or oral statement (1) made before or submitted to a legislative, executive, or judicial body or other governmental proceeding, (2) made in connection with an issue under consideration by such a body, or (3) made in connection with an issue of public concern.\nThe legislative findings say such suits should be resolved quickly and at minimum cost to the citizen.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws §§ 9-33-1, 9-33-2(a), (e)","v":true},{"t":"The only exception: ‘sham’ petitioning or speech, tested two ways","d":"Immunity is lost only if the speech or petition is a sham, meaning it is not genuinely aimed at procuring a favorable government action, result, or outcome. It is a sham only if it is BOTH objectively baseless (no reasonable person could realistically expect success in procuring the government action) AND subjectively baseless (an attempt to use the governmental process itself, rather than its outcome, for its own direct effects).\nThe statute’s sham test is framed around petitioning government. How it applies to speech on a public issue that is not aimed at any government body is not answered by the statutory text; check Rhode Island case law.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-33-2(a)(1)-(2)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: no special motion form, no deadline, automatic discovery stay","d":"Immunity may be raised by an appropriate motion or by other appropriate means under the civil rules. The statute sets no filing deadline and no hearing deadline.\nFiling a motion asserting the immunity automatically stays all discovery until notice of entry of the order ruling on it. The court may allow specified discovery only on motion, after a hearing, for good cause.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-33-2(b)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"Fees are mandatory for the winner; damages if the suit was frivolous or meant to harass","d":"If the court grants the immunity motion, or the speaker or petitioner ultimately prevails at trial, the court must award costs and reasonable attorney’s fees, including those for the motion and related discovery.\nThe court must also award compensatory damages, and may award punitive damages, if the prevailing party shows the claims were frivolous or brought to harass or otherwise inhibit petitioning or speech. Other remedies remain available.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-33-2(d)","v":true},{"t":"Intervention by government and the Attorney General; other protections preserved","d":"The government agency or subdivision to which the petition or speech was directed, or the Attorney General, may intervene to defend or support the speaker. The chapter does not limit other constitutional, statutory, or common-law protections.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws §§ 9-33-3, 9-33-4","v":true},{"t":"Appeal of a denial: the statute is silent","d":"Chapter 9-33 has no appeal provision, and a denial of an immunity motion is not among the interlocutory orders made appealable by R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-24-7 (injunctions, receivers, ordered sales, and new-trial orders after a jury trial). Review before final judgment would have to come by petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court under Article I, Rule 13. Whether the Supreme Court routinely grants such review is a case-law question not answered by the collected texts.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws §§ 9-33-2, 9-24-7; Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 13","v":true},{"t":"Currency check","d":"The legislature’s site shows § 9-33-2 last amended by P.L. 1995, ch. 386, and § 9-33-4 by P.L. 1997, ch. 326. No later amendment (for example, adoption of the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act) appears on the official code site as fetched November 10, 2026.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws §§ 9-33-1 to 9-33-4 (History of Section notes)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice is ‘special and infrequent’ and only for nonresidents","d":"An attorney in good standing in another state, who does not reside in Rhode Island, may be permitted, ‘upon special and infrequent occasion and for good cause shown,’ to take part in a case to the extent the court prescribes. Admission is by written miscellaneous petition presented by a Rhode Island lawyer.\nThe Supreme Court has delegated authority to the Superior, Family, District, and Workers’ Compensation Courts and the Traffic Tribunal, each for its own cases and for agencies and municipal bodies whose decisions are appealed to it. For an agency or municipal proceeding, file the petition in the court that hears appeals from that body.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a); Super.R.Civ.P. 85","v":true},{"t":"Hard cap: 3 cases in 5 years","d":"Unless the Supreme Court waives it for good cause on a separate miscellaneous petition, a nonresident attorney may not appear pro hac vice in more than 3 cases or proceedings in Rhode Island within the 5 years before the petition. The applicant’s certification must state the number of prior Rhode Island pro hac vice appearances in that period and identify each one.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a)(1), (a)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Good cause is about the client, not the lawyer","d":"Good cause is limited to facts affecting the client’s personal or financial welfare. Listed examples: a complex specialty field, a long-standing attorney-client relationship, lack of local counsel with expertise, questions of foreign law, and extensive discovery in another jurisdiction.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a)(i)-(v)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel signs everything and must sit in the courtroom","d":"A Rhode Island lawyer actively practicing from an office in Rhode Island must sign all pleadings, briefs, and other papers and assumes full responsibility for them, for the conduct of the case, and for the visiting lawyer.\nUnless the court permits otherwise, the visiting lawyer may not take part in any proceeding, hearing, or trial unless a Rhode Island lawyer prepared to continue in the visiting lawyer’s absence is present for its entire duration.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a)(2); Super.R.Civ.P. 85","v":true},{"t":"Paperwork: three signatures, one petition per lawyer, filed electronically","d":"The sponsoring Rhode Island attorney signs the miscellaneous petition; the applicant and the client (the named party) each sign certifications on the Clerk’s forms. Each attorney needs a separate petition and filing fee; in an already pending case a motion may be filed in lieu of a petition, with the fee. Petitions are filed through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System. In the Supreme Court, an attorney may not argue without first filing an entry of appearance.\nThe filing-fee amount is not stated in the collected rules; check the Judiciary’s current fee schedule.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a)(3); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 19 and Appendix of Forms","v":true},{"t":"In-house counsel registration (no courtroom work)","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere and employed by an entity at an office in Rhode Island may register as in-house counsel through the Supreme Court Attorney Portal. Registration covers work only for the employer and its affiliates, directors, officers, and employees, and supervised Bar Association pro bono. It does not permit appearing in court or in an agency or municipal proceeding the lawyer expects to be contested without pro hac vice admission. Anyone who has failed the Rhode Island bar exam is ineligible.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(b)","v":true},{"t":"Full admission without the full exam","d":"Lawyers admitted 5 or more years who practiced full time (or taught at an ABA law school) for 5 of the last 10 years take only the essay portion of the Rhode Island exam and need a scaled score of 135. Alternatively, a UBE score of 270 or higher from an exam within 2 years of filing may be transferred. Application fee: $975. Temporary admission is available for lawyers with 1 year of practice working with an approved legal-services program for indigents.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 1A, 1B, 2(b), 5","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are formal and event-specific","d":"A limited-scope engagement requires a written agreement with informed consent. Ghost-writing is not anonymous: a lawyer who only drafts a filing must sign it and disclose the nature and extent of the help, stating that the signature is not an appearance.\nFor court work, the lawyer files an Entry of Limited Appearance only after the client files a pro se appearance, naming the precise court event. More than one event in a civil case needs leave of court and the client’s written consent. A limited appearance cannot be used only to make evidentiary objections, and lawyer and client cannot both argue at the same event. Withdraw by filing a Notice of Withdrawal of Limited Appearance for each event. Opposing counsel may talk directly to the client about matters outside the limited scope.","c":"R.I. Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(d), 4.2","v":true},{"t":"Statewide mandatory e-filing (Article X)","d":"E-filing through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System is mandatory for all parties in the Supreme and Superior Courts, except incarcerated people and attorneys who obtain a Supreme Court waiver; self-represented litigants may opt in. Registering (signing the Usage Agreement) is consent to electronic service.\nFiling is timely if submitted by 11:59 p.m. on the deadline day. A rejected filing that is promptly corrected and resubmitted is deemed filed on the original date for statutory and rule deadlines (the Supreme Court gives 10 days to correct). The /s/ typed signature plus login counts as a personal signature; keep originals of e-filed signed documents for 7 years after disposition.\nCase-initiating papers and subpoenas are never served electronically. eNotification emails are informational only and are not official notice.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. X, R. 1(b), 3, 5(b)-(c), 6, 6.1, 7; Super.R.Civ.P. 1(b); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 1(d), 18(g)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary multijurisdictional practice follows the ABA model","d":"Rule 5.5(c)-(d) tracks the ABA Model Rule safe harbors: work with an actively participating Rhode Island lawyer, matters related to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, related ADR, other work arising from the lawyer’s home practice, and in-house or federally authorized work. A systematic and continuous presence in Rhode Island still requires admission.","c":"R.I. Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Supreme Court Rules Article V (revised October 2023)","d":"Rhode Island’s Rules of Professional Conduct are Article V of the Supreme Court Rules, based on the ABA Model Rules with commentary. Rule 9.1 adds a strong safe harbor: a lawyer who acts in accordance with an opinion of the Supreme Court’s five-member Ethics Advisory Panel, requested by that lawyer about a proposed action, is conclusively presumed to have complied with the Rules. Appendix I adds Standards for Professional Conduct (civility) for lawyers and judges.","c":"R.I. Sup. Ct. Art. V; R. Prof. Conduct 9.1, 9.2, App. I","v":true},{"t":"Fees: writing required, plus quarterly bills","d":"Unlike ABA Rule 1.5(b) (‘preferably in writing’), Rhode Island requires that the scope of representation and the basis or rate of fees and expenses be communicated in writing, before or within a reasonable time after starting, except for a regularly represented client on the same basis. Where the fee is neither fixed nor contingent, bills must go to the client quarterly or as the agreement provides.","c":"R.I. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: much narrower exceptions than the ABA rule","d":"Rhode Island permits disclosure only to prevent the client from committing a criminal act likely to result in imminent death or substantial bodily harm, for lawyer-client disputes and defense of claims against the lawyer, to get ethics advice, and to comply with law or court order.\nThere is no counterpart to the ABA exceptions for client crimes or frauds causing substantial financial injury using the lawyer’s services, for detecting conflicts when changing firms, or for preventing death or serious harm not caused by a client crime.","c":"R.I. R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(b)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.8: no client-sex rule and no humanitarian-gift exception","d":"Rhode Island’s Rule 1.8 has no counterpart to ABA Rule 1.8(j) (sexual relations with a client); its paragraph (j) is the imputation rule. Its financial-assistance rule allows advancing costs and paying costs for indigent clients but lacks the ABA’s 2020 exception for modest gifts of living expenses to indigent pro bono clients. Other conduct rules may still reach such behavior.","c":"R.I. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e), (j)","v":true},{"t":"Advertising: file ads with Disciplinary Counsel within 48 hours","d":"Rhode Island keeps filing rules the ABA dropped. Copies of print ads (except yellow pages), radio recordings, and TV videotapes must go to Supreme Court Disciplinary Counsel before or within 48 hours of first dissemination, and the lawyer must keep a copy with a record of when and where it ran for 3 years. Targeted written or electronic solicitations must say ‘Advertising Material’ on the envelope and at the start and end of recorded or electronic messages, and copies go to Disciplinary Counsel and are kept 3 years. Ads promising ‘no fee if no recovery’ must state conspicuously whether the client pays costs or expenses regardless of outcome, and a lawyer who advertises a practice area but refers out most of those cases must carry a prescribed disclaimer. Specialist claims require certification by an approved or ABA-accredited body, naming it, plus a prescribed disclaimer that the Supreme Court does not certify specialists.","c":"R.I. R. Prof. Conduct 7.2(b), (e), (f), 7.3(c)-(d), 7.4(d)","v":true},{"t":"Discrimination is folded into 8.4(d); recordkeeping rule 1.19","d":"Rhode Island did not adopt ABA Rule 8.4(g). Instead, 8.4(d) defines conduct prejudicial to the administration of justice to include harmful or discriminatory treatment of litigants, jurors, witnesses, lawyers, and others based on listed characteristics.\nRule 1.19 (no ABA counterpart in the Model Rules) requires keeping trust and operating account records, retainer agreements, client statements, and bills for 7 years.","c":"R.I. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(d), 1.19","v":true}],"style":"State of Rhode Island","home":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"20 days to appeal, and the Supreme Court cannot extend it","d":"A civil notice of appeal, with the $150 filing fee, must be filed in the trial court within 20 days of entry of the judgment, order, or decree (not 30). Only the trial court may extend, for excusable neglect, by no more than 30 days past the original deadline; the Supreme Court has no power to extend the time to appeal.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 20(b)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions are due in 10 days, and only some stop the appeal clock","d":"Renewed motions for judgment as a matter of law, motions to amend findings, new-trial motions, and motions to alter or amend must be served within 10 days after entry of judgment, and the time cannot be extended. Only those four motions restart the 20-day appeal period. A Rule 60(b) motion does not.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)"},{"t":"District Court civil appeals: 2 business days","d":"To get a de novo trial in Superior Court after a District Court civil judgment, a party must file a written claim of appeal with the District Court clerk within 2 days after entry of judgment, excluding weekends and legal holidays, and pay the costs, including a $50 attorney’s fee for the other side, at that time.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-12-10"},{"t":"12% interest from accrual changes settlement math","d":"Prejudgment interest at 12% a year runs from the date the cause of action accrued and is added by the clerk; post-judgment interest is also 12%, accruing on the prejudgment interest as well. Each year between accrual and judgment adds about 12% of the damages award.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-21-10(a)"},{"t":"Arbitration certificate within 3 days and a 30-day rejection window","d":"Money cases of $100,000 or less are diverted to court-annexed arbitration. Counsel must file the arbitration certificate within 3 days after the last responsive pleading. Missing the 30-day deadline to file a written rejection (with $300) after the award is filed means judgment enters on the award.","c":"Super. Ct. R. Governing Arbitration of Civil Actions 1(a), (e), 5"},{"t":"Pro hac vice: 3 cases per 5 years, and local counsel in the room","d":"Nonresident lawyers are limited to 3 Rhode Island pro hac vice cases in 5 years unless the Supreme Court waives it, and admission is for ‘special and infrequent’ occasions based on client need. Rhode Island counsel must sign every paper and attend every hearing and trial day, ready to proceed alone, unless excused.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. II, R. 9(a); Super.R.Civ.P. 85"},{"t":"Slander has a 1-year statute of limitations","d":"Claims for words spoken must be filed within 1 year after the words were spoken. Personal-injury claims get 3 years.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-1-14(a)-(b)"},{"t":"Uncontested-motion trap: object 3 days before the hearing","d":"Motions to amend, compel, for protective orders, to consolidate, to add third parties, for Rule 35 exams, to enlarge time after a deadline, and to assign are granted as a matter of course and never reach the calendar unless a written objection stating particular grounds is served and filed at least 3 days before the hearing. The extra day for electronic service does not apply.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 7(b)(3)"},{"t":"Discovery runs on Rhode Island numbers, not federal ones","d":"No initial disclosures. Interrogatory answers and document responses are due in 40 days (a defendant gets 60 days after service of process for document requests), with a 30-interrogatory cap. A plaintiff needs leave to serve interrogatories within 60 days after service of process. Untimely interrogatory objections are waived. Supplemental interrogatory answers are due no later than 30 days before trial. An order compelling discovery can provide for automatic dismissal or default if not obeyed within 30 days.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 26, 33(a)-(c), 34(b)(2), 37(a)(4)(A)"},{"t":"Do not plead a dollar amount in injury cases","d":"In actions for personal injury, property damage, or wrongful death, the complaint must not state the amount claimed, only that it is enough for the court’s jurisdiction.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 8(a)"},{"t":"Miss the Rule 12A statement and the appeal is conditionally dismissed","d":"The Rule 12A statement is due 20 days after the appeal is docketed. If it is late, the Clerk enters a conditional dismissal that becomes final unless the statement or an extension motion is filed within 10 days. A late appellee counter-statement or brief leads to a conditional default barring further filings and argument.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(1)-(2), 18A"},{"t":"Supreme Court briefs need 9 paper copies within 5 days","d":"Even with mandatory e-filing, parties must deliver 9 paper copies of Rule 12A statements and briefs (6 of appendices, writ petitions, reargument petitions, and supporting memoranda) within 5 days after the Clerk accepts the electronic filing. Briefs must be in 14-point Times New Roman, double-spaced, with a signed word-count certification and the Clerk’s ‘Checklist for Filing Briefs’; a brief without the checklist is rejected.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(f), 18(b), (h)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory substitution of judge; recusal under Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11","d":"None of the collected rules provides a peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge. Disqualification is for cause under Rule 2.11 of the Rhode Island Code of Judicial Conduct (Article VI, revised May 2024): whenever the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned, including listed grounds. Rhode Island’s version adds a judge who ‘has demonstrated an actual bias towards a party’s lawyer.’ The duty applies whether or not a motion is filed; the rule commentary notes ‘recusal’ and ‘disqualification’ are used interchangeably.\nRemittal: except for personal bias, the judge may disclose the ground on the record, and the parties and lawyers may agree, outside the judge’s presence, to waive it; the agreement goes in the record.","c":"R.I. Sup. Ct. Art. VI, Code of Judicial Conduct R. 2.11(A), (C) & cmts. [1]-[2]","v":true},{"t":"No judge-shopping on ex parte TROs and receivers","d":"If one judicial officer denies an ex parte TRO or an ex parte temporary receiver, the application may not be made to another judicial officer without a material change in circumstances, and the first judge notes the action in the file.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 65(b), 66(b)","v":true},{"t":"Successor judge mid-trial","d":"If a judicial officer cannot continue, another may proceed after certifying familiarity with the record; in a non-jury matter the successor must recall, on request, any available witness whose material testimony is disputed.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 63","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"R.I. Const. art. I, § 21 | Broader than federal: not established here","d":"The Rhode Island Constitution protects freedom of speech, press, assembly, and petition. The collected texts do not include the Constitution or case law, so whether the Rhode Island Supreme Court reads these clauses more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. The anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects speech and petitioning under both the U.S. and Rhode Island constitutions.","c":"R.I. Const. art. I, § 21 (not in collected texts); R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-33-2(a)","v":false},{"t":"One-year limitations period for slander","d":"Actions for words spoken must be brought within 1 year after the words were spoken. Written defamation is not addressed by this section; check which limitations period applies to libel.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-1-14(a)","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Mandatory court-annexed arbitration for claims of $100,000 or less","d":"Superior Court civil actions seeking money of $100,000 or less in total (excluding interest, costs, and fees), and District Court appeals, go to court-annexed arbitration, with listed exceptions (class actions, substantial injunctive or declaratory claims, family, real-estate title, estates, landlord-tenant, book accounts, formal and special cause matters). Within 3 days after the last responsive pleading, counsel must file a Court Annexed Arbitration Certificate stating the claim amount. The hearing must conclude within 240 days of the arbitrator’s appointment.\nA dissatisfied party gets trial as of right only by filing a written rejection within 30 days after the award is filed, with a $300 fee; otherwise judgment enters on the award.","c":"Super. Ct. R. Governing Arbitration of Civil Actions 1(a), (e), 5","v":true},{"t":"12% prejudgment and post-judgment interest","d":"In any civil action with a verdict or decision for money damages, the clerk adds interest at 12% a year from the date the cause of action accrued, and post-judgment interest also runs at 12% on both principal and prejudgment interest. It does not apply to contracts that already set interest. In medical-malpractice cases against listed providers, interest runs from written notice of the claim or filing, whichever is first.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-21-10","v":true},{"t":"Supreme Court ‘show cause’ calendar and prebriefing statements","d":"Most appeals do not get full briefing first. Within 20 days after docketing, the appellant files a Rule 12A statement of the case (3,000 words maximum, with the judgment and the trial decision attached); the appellee’s counter-statement follows within 15 days. A single justice may then order the case decided on the show cause calendar (10 minutes of argument a side before a panel of at least 3 justices) or assign it for full briefing.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A, 24(b)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Rhode Island Supreme Court Rules, Article I (Appellate Procedure), revised February 17, 2026; Article X (Rules Governing Electronic Filing), revised September 2023; Superior Court Rules of Civil Procedure (February 2024 compilation), Rules 50, 52, 54(b), 59, 60, 62; Superior Court Rules of Practice 2.9; R.I. Gen. Laws §§ 9-12-10, 9-24-1, 9-24-7 (official text from webserver.rilegislature.gov). Official texts from courts.ri.gov; summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Rhode Island: one appellate court, a 20-day clock, and a prebriefing screen","d":"Rhode Island has no intermediate appellate court. Appeals from final judgments of the Superior Court and Family Court go straight to the Supreme Court by a notice of appeal filed in the trial court within 20 days of entry of judgment. Everything else (most interlocutory orders, Workers’ Compensation and District Court rulings, agency matters) reaches the Supreme Court, if at all, by petition for certiorari or another extraordinary writ under Rule 13.\nAfter the record arrives, most civil appeals are screened: eligible cases go to the Appellate Mediation Program, then the parties file short Rule 12A prebriefing statements, and a single justice decides whether the case is decided on the expedited ‘show cause’ calendar or assigned for full briefing and argument. Filing is electronic, but paper copies of statements and briefs are still required."},"groups":[["What can be appealed, and how",[{"t":"Final judgments: notice of appeal in the trial court","d":"A party aggrieved by a final judgment, decree, or order of the Superior Court may appeal to the Supreme Court within the time set by the rules. The notice of appeal is filed with the trial-court clerk, names the appealing parties and the judgment or part appealed from (use the Judiciary’s current form), and is served on every party’s attorney or an unrepresented party at the last known address. Only a timely notice and the filing fee are jurisdictional; other missteps are grounds for such action as the court deems appropriate, including dismissal.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-24-1; Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 3(a), (c)-(d)"},{"t":"Interlocutory orders appealable by statute","d":"An appeal lies, as from a final judgment and with precedence in the Supreme Court, from Superior Court interlocutory orders or judgments that grant or continue an injunction, appoint a receiver, order a sale of real or personal property, or order or deny a new trial after a jury trial.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-24-7"},{"t":"Partial final judgment under Rule 54(b)","d":"In multi-claim or multi-party cases, an order resolving fewer than all claims is appealable only if the trial court expressly determines there is no just reason for delay and expressly directs entry of judgment. Otherwise it can be revised until final judgment. Cases sent to appellate mediation with incomplete judgments must obtain a Rule 54(b) judgment.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 54(b); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 35(d)(2)"},{"t":"Other interlocutory review: petition for certiorari or another writ","d":"Review of other orders is sought by petition to the Supreme Court for an extraordinary writ. The petition must state the facts, explain with particularity why relief is unavailable in any other court or through other appellate processes (including whether a notice of appeal was filed and why it is not enough), and attach the order and essential record parts, with a supporting memorandum and a $150 fee per petitioner. Opposition is due 20 days after service; reply 10 days after service of the opposition. A respondent may not file a motion to dismiss or quash. Denial without more is not a merits ruling. If granted, the case proceeds under Rule 12A. No rule fixes a deadline to file a petition.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 13(a)-(e)"},{"t":"Post-judgment orders","d":"Orders entered after judgment may be appealed in the same manner, as near as may be, as judgments. An appeal from the judgment also preserves claims of error in rulings on reserved directed-verdict motions, motions to amend findings or the judgment, new-trial motions, and motions in arrest of judgment.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 30"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"The Supreme Court may answer questions of Rhode Island law certified by U.S. courts or the Superior, Family, District, or Workers’ Compensation Courts if the question may be determinative and there is no controlling precedent. Unless ordered otherwise, the plaintiff files the opening brief. In the Superior Court, a party’s motion to certify under a statute must be served at least 10 days before the hearing.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 6; Super.R.Civ.P. 72"},{"t":"Below the Superior Court: District Court and magistrates","d":"District Court civil judgments are not appealed to the Supreme Court; a party claims an appeal for a de novo trial in the Superior Court within 2 days after entry of judgment, excluding weekends and legal holidays, paying costs including a $50 attorney’s fee for the other side. A Superior Court magistrate’s decision is appealed to a Superior Court justice by notice filed within 20 days of entry (no fee); the appellant must file the transcript within 60 days of entry or the appeal is dismissed absent good cause.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-12-10; Super. Ct. R. Prac. 2.9(b), (e), (g)"}]],["Timing: the notice of appeal and what stops the clock",[{"t":"20 days from entry, with a $150 fee","d":"In a civil case the notice of appeal, with a $150 filing fee, must be filed with the trial-court clerk within 20 days of the date of entry of the judgment, order, or decree. A judgment is entered when it is set forth and signed by the clerk. A notice filed after the decision but before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. Joint appellants each pay $150. The State is exempt from the fee. An indigent appellant must petition the trial court for a fee waiver within the appeal time; filing the petition (with the proposed notice) stops the clock, which restarts from denial.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 5(a)-(c); Super.R.Civ.P. 58"},{"t":"Tolling motions: only four, and each is due in 10 days","d":"The appeal time is terminated for all parties by a timely Superior Court motion under Rule 50(b) (renewed judgment as a matter of law), 52(b) (amend findings), 59 (alter or amend judgment), or 59 (new trial), or comparable Family Court motions. The full 20 days runs again from entry of the order deciding the motion. Each of these motions must be served within 10 days after entry of judgment, and that time cannot be extended. A Rule 60(b) motion is not on the list and does not toll.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a); Super.R.Civ.P. 6(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 60(b)"},{"t":"Extension only from the trial court, only for excusable neglect, at most 30 days","d":"On a showing of excusable neglect, the trial court may extend the appeal time for any party by up to 30 days from the end of the original period, before or after it expires (after expiry, by motion with notice). The Supreme Court may not extend the time to file a notice of appeal.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 20(b)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"After a timely notice of appeal, any other party may file a notice within 20 days after the first notice was filed (or deemed filed), or within the ordinary appeal time, whichever ends later. In consolidated cases or cross-appeals, the first to file is the appellant and files the opening brief; the second files one brief that responds and presents its cross-appeal; the appellant replies; the cross-appellant may reply on the cross-appeal issues. Same-day filings make the plaintiff below the appellant. Seek changes to this order before the Rule 12A conference.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 16(d)"}]],["Transcript, record, and docketing",[{"t":"Order the transcript within 20 days","d":"Within 20 days after filing the notice of appeal the appellant orders the needed transcript. If less than all of it is ordered, the appellant must file and serve a description of the parts and a statement of the points to be raised; the appellee then orders any additional parts. If no transcript exists, the appellant may prepare a statement of the evidence, with objections due 10 days after service, settled by the trial court. For mediation-eligible civil appeals the ordering deadline is 60 days from the notice of appeal.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 10(b)-(c), 35(e)"},{"t":"Record transmitted within 60 days; extensions capped at 90","d":"The record, including the transcript, must be transmitted to the Supreme Court within 60 days after the notice of appeal (for mediation cases, 60 days after the transcript is ordered). The trial court may extend, on a request made before the deadline and showing causes beyond the appellant’s control or excusable neglect, but not beyond 90 days from the first notice of appeal; beyond that, only the Supreme Court can grant relief. An appellee may move in the trial court to dismiss for failure to transmit. Until docketing, the trial court and Supreme Court share supervision; after docketing, the Supreme Court controls.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 11(a), (c), (f), 12(c), 35(e)"},{"t":"After docketing","d":"Report errors in the case title within 7 days after notice of docketing. Motions to correct the record are due within 20 days after docketing. Each attorney must file a written appearance (name, address, email, bar number, phone) by the Rule 12A deadline or, where Rule 12A does not apply, the brief deadline; no one may argue without one.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12(b), (d), 19"}]],["Appellate mediation",[{"t":"Most counseled civil appeals are screened for mediation","d":"All civil appeals are eligible except post-conviction and habeas cases, prisoner cases, cases with an unrepresented party (unless included by order), Family Court appeals, juvenile cases, and petitions for extraordinary relief. If a case is eligible, the Mediation Statement (Part I case information, served on opponents; Part II confidential, filed only with the program) is due 20 days from the Notice of Eligibility, with a statement that counsel has full settlement authority. The appellate rules are not suspended except for the extended transcript and record deadlines. Non-participation or bad faith may bring sanctions, including a bar on further filings.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 35(b), (d)-(e), (g)-(h)"}]],["Prebriefing: Rule 12A statements and the show cause calendar",[{"t":"Statement of the case within 20 days of docketing","d":"Within 20 days after the appeal is docketed (or after the record arrives on a granted certiorari petition), the appellant files a statement of the case and summary of issues, no more than 3,000 words, with copies of the judgment or order and the trial court’s bench or written decision; the Clerk rejects a statement missing them. The counter-statement (3,000 words) is due 15 days after the statement is filed. Replies need leave. Each party’s documents go in a separate appendix.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(1)-(2), (5)"},{"t":"Single-justice conference and routing","d":"A single justice may hold a conference (counsel must come with settlement authority in civil cases) and then order the parties to show cause why the appeal should not be decided summarily, refer it to the full Court, place it on the regular calendar for full briefing, consolidate, or remand. Show cause supplemental statements (3,000 words) are due 20 days after the show cause order, with the response 10 days later. Show cause arguments are 10 minutes a side plus 2 minutes of rebuttal before at least 3 justices, and the Court may dismiss, reverse, modify, remand, or send the case to full briefing.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(3)-(4), (7)-(8), 24(b)"}]],["Full briefing, length, and form",[{"t":"Briefing schedule: 40 / 40 / 20","d":"The appellant’s brief is due 40 days after the Clerk gives notice that the case is assigned to the regular calendar for full briefing; the appellee’s brief 40 days after the appellant’s is filed; any reply 20 days after the appellee’s brief. No further briefs or post-argument memoranda without leave. Supplemental authorities may be cited by a short ‘Citation of Supplemental Authority’ without argument; a response is due within 10 days and no reply is allowed.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(a)-(c), (e)"},{"t":"Contents, and what is waived","d":"The brief must include facts and prior proceedings with record and appendix cites, a specification of errors with page cites, numbered questions each with a standard of review, argument with authority, a specific conclusion, and an alphabetical index of authorities. Errors not claimed, questions not raised, and points not made are ordinarily treated as waived. Use parties’ names or descriptive terms rather than ‘appellant’ and ‘appellee.’ The appellant files a separate appendix (docket entries, relevant pleadings, the ruling appealed, and key transcript pages with the cover sheet and witness index).","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(a)-(b), (i), 17"},{"t":"Word limits and format","d":"Briefs: 15,000 words; reply briefs: 7,500 words (typed or handwritten briefs: 50 and 25 pages). Rule 12A statements and show cause statements: 3,000 words. Headings, footnotes, and quotations count; cover, tables, statement of issues, signature blocks, and certifications do not. All papers: 8.5 by 11 inches, double-spaced, Times New Roman at least 14-point (footnotes too, single-spaced), 1-inch margins, page numbers centered at the bottom. Every statement and brief needs the signed word-count certification in the rule’s form. Leave to exceed limits must be granted before the brief is filed; an oversized brief is not accepted provisionally. Briefs must also be filed with the signed ‘Checklist for Filing Briefs’ or the Clerk rejects them.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(1), 16(f), 18(b)"},{"t":"Amicus briefs","d":"Allowed with all parties’ written consent or by leave, except the United States and the State of Rhode Island need neither. A motion must state the amicus’s interest and why the brief is desirable; the brief is due with the brief of the party it supports unless leave is granted. Amicus argument is allowed only for extraordinary reasons.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(h)"},{"t":"Citing cases","d":"Unpublished orders have no precedential effect and may not be cited. Cite an electronic database only when the case is not yet in the bound national reporter, and a Lexis cite must be accompanied by the Westlaw cite.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18(l)"}]],["Extensions, defaults, and dismissal",[{"t":"Two 30-day extensions from the Clerk","d":"The Clerk’s office may grant one 30-day extension on motion, then one more 30-day extension (two more in criminal cases) unless an objection is filed within 7 days, in which case the Court decides. Further extensions require a Supreme Court order for good cause. These limits do not apply to reargument petitions or cures of conditional dismissal. In an expedited appeal, no extensions without the Court’s express permission. Add 1 day to periods running from electronic or mail service.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 20(b), (d), 28(e)"},{"t":"Conditional dismissals and defaults","d":"A late Rule 12A statement or appellant’s brief leads to a conditional dismissal; a late counter-statement or appellee’s brief to a conditional default barring further filings and argument. Each is cured only by filing the paper (or, for 12A papers, an extension motion) within 10 days. The Clerk also conditionally dismisses any case with no party action for at least 90 days while no Court action is pending; it is reinstated if a show cause statement is filed within 20 days. A party that obtained a stay or abeyance must report status every 60 days. The Court may impose monetary sanctions on attorneys.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18A, 18B(2)-(3)"},{"t":"Voluntary dismissal","d":"Before oral argument is scheduled the appellant may withdraw the appeal by written notice; afterward, dismissal is by stipulation signed by the parties.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18B(1)"}]],["Stays pending appeal",[{"t":"Automatic 20-day stay, then a supersedeas bond","d":"No execution issues until 20 days after entry of judgment or until the appeal time expires, but injunctions and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered. A stay pending appeal is obtained with a supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it (the State is exempt). Seek a stay or injunction pending appeal in the trial court first; a motion to the Supreme Court must show that was impracticable or was denied (with reasons), include the notice of appeal and relevant record, and be supported by affidavits if facts are disputed. The Supreme Court may require a bond. Stays of trial-court proceedings on aspects of the case not on appeal also start in the trial court.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 62(a), (c)-(d); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 7(a), 8, 11(f)"}]],["Argument, decision, and reargument",[{"t":"Oral argument","d":"Full-calendar cases get 30 minutes a side, with 10 minutes of rebuttal for the appellant; only one attorney argues per party without leave. The Court hears arguments September through May. Reading argument from a prepared text is disfavored. If counsel does not appear, the Court may decide on the papers.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 22(b), (f), 24"},{"t":"Reargument: 10 days, no extensions from the Clerk","d":"A petition for reargument, with a memorandum of grounds, must be filed within 10 days after the decision is filed, including a request for reargument before the full court after an evenly divided decision. Opposition is due within 10 days after receipt. The Clerk’s extension authority does not apply to reargument petitions. There is no further state appellate review: the Supreme Court is the court of last resort.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 20(b), 25"}]],["Filing and service in the Supreme Court",[{"t":"Mandatory e-filing, plus paper copies","d":"All parties must e-file through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System, except incarcerated people and attorneys with a waiver; self-represented litigants may opt in. Filing is timely only if received by the deadline; the e-filing system accepts filings until 11:59 p.m. on the due date, and the Clerk’s file mark is the only proof of filing time. Each document goes as a separate file. The Clerk may reject for listed defects (including fillable PDFs, wrong case, wrong caption, wrong filing code); a rejected filing must be corrected within 10 days of notice. Within 5 days after acceptance, file 9 paper copies of Rule 12A statements and briefs and 6 of appendices, writ petitions, reargument petitions, and supporting memoranda, matching the electronic version exactly.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 1(d), 18(a), (f)-(h); Art. X, R. 5(b)-(c)"},{"t":"Service, motions, and emergencies","d":"Serve every filing on all parties (on counsel if represented) at or before filing, with proof of service. Motions need a supporting memorandum; opposition within 10 days of service and reply within 10 days after that. Sealed filings require a motion when filed. Emergency motions go through the Office of the Administrative Assistant to the Chief Justice, with a certificate of efforts to notify others, and ordinarily only after a notice of appeal or petition has been filed. Requests to be excused from attendance must be emailed at least 30 days ahead absent emergency, and do not excuse filing deadlines.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18(c)-(e), 26A(b), 28, 34"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal, civil (with $150 fee)","d":"20 days from entry of the judgment, order, or decree (set forth and signed by the clerk).\nFiled in the trial court. Restarts from entry of an order deciding a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59 motion. The Supreme Court cannot extend it.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a), 5(a)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment, order, or decree)","note":"Filed in the trial court. Restarts from entry of an order deciding a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59 motion. The Supreme Court cannot extend it."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal / other party’s notice of appeal","d":"20 days from filing (or deemed filing) of the first notice of appeal, or the ordinary appeal period if longer.\nWhichever period ends later.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing)","note":"Whichever period ends later."}},{"t":"Extension of appeal time for excusable neglect","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nGranted only by the trial court; maximum 30 days.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal period)","note":"Granted only by the trial court; maximum 30 days."}},{"t":"Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59 post-trial motion (tolls the appeal period)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended (Super.R.Civ.P. 6(b)). Rule 60(b) motions do not toll.","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended (Super.R.Civ.P. 6(b)). Rule 60(b) motions do not toll."}},{"t":"Order transcript (and file statement of parts and points if partial)","d":"20 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\n60 days from the notice of appeal for mediation-eligible civil appeals (R. 35(e)).","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 10(b)(1)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"60 days from the notice of appeal for mediation-eligible civil appeals (R. 35(e))."}},{"t":"Transmit record to the Supreme Court","d":"60 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nTrial court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice of appeal. Mediation cases: 60 days from ordering the transcript (R. 35(e)).","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 11(a), (c)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Trial court may extend to no more than 90 days from the first notice of appeal. Mediation cases: 60 days from ordering the transcript (R. 35(e))."}},{"t":"Mediation Statement (Parts I and II)","d":"20 days from date of the Notice of Eligibility.\nPart II is confidential and goes only to the mediation program.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 35(d)(1)-(2)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (date of the Notice of Eligibility)","note":"Part II is confidential and goes only to the mediation program."}},{"t":"Report errors in docketed case title","d":"7 days from notice of docketing.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12(b)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (notice of docketing)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion to correct the record","d":"20 days from docketing.\nOr further time the Court allows.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12(d)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (docketing)","note":"Or further time the Court allows."}},{"t":"Rule 12A statement of the case (3,000 words)","d":"20 days from docketing of the appeal (or receipt of the record on granted certiorari).\nMust attach the judgment and the trial court’s decision. Late filing leads to conditional dismissal (R. 18A(1)).","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(1)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (docketing of the appeal)","note":"Must attach the judgment and the trial court’s decision. Late filing leads to conditional dismissal (R. 18A(1))."}},{"t":"Rule 12A counter-statement (3,000 words)","d":"15 days from filing of the appellant’s statement.\nLate filing leads to conditional default (R. 18A(2)).","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(2)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellant’s statement)","note":"Late filing leads to conditional default (R. 18A(2))."}},{"t":"Show cause supplemental statement (3,000 words)","d":"20 days from issuance of the show cause order.\nResponding party’s counter-statement 10 days after that statement is filed or was due.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 12A(4)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (issuance of the show cause order)","note":"Responding party’s counter-statement 10 days after that statement is filed or was due."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief (15,000 words)","d":"40 days from clerk’s notice of assignment to the regular calendar for full briefing.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(a), (f)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s notice of assignment to the regular calendar for full briefing)","note":""}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief (15,000 words)","d":"40 days from filing of the appellant’s brief.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(b), (f)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief (7,500 words)","d":"20 days from filing of the appellee’s brief.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(c), (f)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellee’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Paper copies of Rule 12A statements and briefs (9) or appendices, petitions, memoranda (6)","d":"5 days from clerk’s acceptance of the electronic filing.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18(h)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s acceptance of the electronic filing)","note":""}},{"t":"Correct and resubmit a rejected filing","d":"10 days from service of the rejection notice.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18(g), (i)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the rejection notice)","note":""}},{"t":"Cure a conditional dismissal or default for a late statement or brief","d":"10 days from entry of the conditional order.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18A","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the conditional order)","note":""}},{"t":"Show cause after conditional dismissal for 90 days’ inactivity","d":"20 days from entry of the conditional order of dismissal.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 18B(2)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the conditional order of dismissal)","note":""}},{"t":"Objection to a second Clerk extension","d":"7 days from filing of the motion to extend.\nEach Clerk extension is 30 days.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 20(b)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the motion to extend)","note":"Each Clerk extension is 30 days."}},{"t":"Opposition to a motion","d":"10 days from service of the motion and memorandum.\nReply 10 days after service of the opposition (R. 28(c)).","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 28(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion and memorandum)","note":"Reply 10 days after service of the opposition (R. 28(c))."}},{"t":"Memorandum opposing a petition for extraordinary writ / certiorari","d":"20 days from service of the petition and memorandum.\nReply 10 days after service of the opposition (R. 13(c)). No rule deadline to file the petition itself.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 13(b)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition and memorandum)","note":"Reply 10 days after service of the opposition (R. 13(c)). No rule deadline to file the petition itself."}},{"t":"Response to Citation of Supplemental Authority","d":"10 days from filing of the citation.\nNo reply allowed.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 16(e)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the citation)","note":"No reply allowed."}},{"t":"Petition for reargument","d":"10 days from filing of the decision.\nOpposition within 10 days after receipt (R. 25(b)). Not extendable by the Clerk.","c":"Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 25(a)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the decision)","note":"Opposition within 10 days after receipt (R. 25(b)). Not extendable by the Clerk."}},{"t":"Automatic stay of execution","d":"20 days from entry of judgment.\nOr until the appeal time expires. Injunctions and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered; afterward a supersedeas bond is needed (R. 62(d)).","c":"Super.R.Civ.P. 62(a)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Or until the appeal time expires. Injunctions and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered; afterward a supersedeas bond is needed (R. 62(d))."}},{"t":"District Court civil appeal for de novo trial in Superior Court","d":"2 days from entry of District Court judgment.\nExcludes Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays; costs including a $50 attorney’s fee paid when the appeal is claimed.","c":"R.I. Gen. Laws § 9-12-10","due":{"n":2,"from":"the trigger date (entry of District Court judgment)","note":"Excludes Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays; costs including a $50 attorney’s fee paid when the appeal is claimed."}},{"t":"Appeal from a Superior Court magistrate to a Superior Court justice","d":"20 days from entry of the magistrate’s judgment, order, or decree.\nTranscript must be filed within 60 days of entry or the appeal is dismissed absent good cause (R. Prac. 2.9(g)).","c":"Super. Ct. R. Prac. 2.9(e)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the magistrate’s judgment, order, or decree)","note":"Transcript must be filed within 60 days of entry or the appeal is dismissed absent good cause (R. Prac. 2.9(g))."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.courts.ri.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Rhode Island Superior Court Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules and mandatory electronic filing","s":"Govern all civil suits in the Superior Court, at law or in equity (exceptions in Rule 81); cited as Super.R.Civ.P. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 1(b) makes e-filing through the Judiciary’s Electronic Filing System (EFS) mandatory for all parties except incarcerated people and attorneys granted a waiver under Art. X, Rule 3(c); self-represented litigants may opt in. Case-initiating papers must include the Language Assistance Notice, related documents go up as separate files, and Rule 1(b)(4) lists the only grounds on which the clerk may reject a filing (for example a fillable PDF, wrong case, wrong caption, wrong filing code).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action is commenced by filing the complaint, all other required documents, and the fees. Incarcerated people, waived attorneys, and non-e-filing self-represented litigants may mail the papers with the fee to the clerk or file at the clerk’s office.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Process, attachment, trustee process, arrest","s":"The court generates the summons in the EFS after accepting the complaint. Service by an authorized officer under R.I. Gen. Laws Title 9, ch. 5, or any non-party age 18 or older; the summons, complaint, and Language Assistance Notice are served together. Waiver of service: at least 30 days to return (60 if abroad), answer due 60 days after the request was sent (90 if abroad), and unjustified refusal shifts service costs. Out-of-state service on individuals may be by registered or certified mail, return receipt, or overnight carrier with a signed receipt. Service must be made within 120 days of commencement or the court dismisses without prejudice or sets a deadline (extended for good cause). Unlike the federal rule, Rule 4(m)-(n) also governs prejudgment attachment and trustee process (only on motion, never ex parte, on a showing of probable success and need for security) and writs of arrest and ne exeat.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Later papers are served electronically on Registered Users through the EFS with the Rule 1(b)(1)(B) certificate of service; others by hand delivery, mail (complete on mailing), or court-ordered means. Interrogatories, document requests, requests for admission, responses, deposition notices, and transcripts are not filed until used or ordered, but relevant parts must be filed with Rule 26(c), 37, and 56 motions. If a party fails to file a required paper within 5 days after service, the court may order it filed and then strike it.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day; include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or legal holiday. Extensions before expiry for cause; after expiry only for excusable neglect; no extension of the Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e), or 60(b) times. Written motions and notice of hearing must be served at least 10 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits by 1 day before. Add 1 day (not 3 as in the federal rule) after electronic or mail service.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint and answer; a jury demand is a separate pleading. Certain motions (to assign, consolidate, enlarge time after expiry, third-party complaint, amend, Rule 35 exam, protective order or compel, wage or non-wage attachment) must be served and filed 10 days before hearing and are granted as a matter of course unless a written objection stating particular grounds is served and filed at least 3 days before the hearing; Rule 6(d)’s extra day does not apply. A motion to compel must state the number of days for compliance. Remote hearings may be requested by the movant when filing or by others within 5 days of service.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Short and plain statement plus a demand for judgment. In personal-injury, property-damage, and wrongful-death actions the complaint must not state an amount, only that it is enough for jurisdiction. Affirmative defenses listed as in the federal rule (including injury by fellow servant); unanswered averments other than damages are admitted.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Fraud and mistake with particularity; capacity challenged by specific negative averment; conditions precedent pleaded generally but denied with particularity; special damages stated specifically.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption must show the court, the county, the title, the file number, and the Rule 7(a) designation; numbered paragraphs; exhibits are part of the pleading.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of pleadings, motions, and other papers; sanctions","s":"Signed by at least one attorney of record with address, email, bar number, and phone (/s/ signature under Art. X, Rule 7). The signature certifies reasonable inquiry and proper purpose. Unlike the current federal rule, there is no 21-day safe harbor in the text; sanctions may be imposed on motion or on the court’s own initiative, including reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"Answer within 20 days after service (or 20 days after last publication); 60 days after a waiver request was sent (90 if abroad). Answer to cross-claim and reply to counterclaim: 20 days. After denial of a Rule 12 motion, respond within 10 days of notice. Seven listed defenses may be raised by motion, including failure to join an indispensable party; a Rule 12(b)(6) motion or defense must include a short, concise statement of grounds. Motion to strike within 20 days if no responsive pleading is permitted. A more definite statement must be supplied within 10 days of the order.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"Compulsory counterclaim rule as in the federal rule, with a Rhode Island exception: a claim need not be pleaded as a counterclaim if the opposing party’s claim is for damage arising from the pleader’s ownership, maintenance, operation, use, or control of a motor vehicle. In an action entered in Superior Court on appeal from the District Court, counterclaims are not compulsory. Omitted counterclaims may be added by leave.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may serve a third-party complaint without leave if it is filed no later than 10 days after serving the original answer (the federal rule allows 14 days); otherwise leave on motion. The third-party plaintiff must tell the third-party defendant whether and when the case is assigned for trial.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"One amendment as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar (narrower than the federal 21-day window after a Rule 12 motion). Otherwise by leave, freely given, or written consent; in the EFS the amended pleading is attached as an exhibit to the motion. Respond within the time left or 10 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. Relation back for a changed party if notice and knowledge exist within the Rule 4 service period (120 days).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pre-trial procedure; formulating issues","s":"Discretionary pretrial conference on issues, amendments, admissions, number of expert witnesses, and electronically stored information; the resulting order controls unless modified to prevent manifest injustice. There is no federal-style mandatory scheduling order in the text. A trial judge may hold a conference on the day trial starts.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Real party in interest, with time allowed for ratification or substitution; a subrogated insurer may sue in the insured’s name. Capacity of individuals and associations follows Rhode Island law; corporations, the law of incorporation. Guardian ad litem or next friend for minors and incompetent persons.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"Unlimited joinder of claims against an opponent; a money claim and a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance may be joined. A liability insurer may not be joined in a personal-injury or property-damage action unless it is directly liable to and directly suable by the injured person.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Federal-style required-party joinder and the four-factor ‘equity and good conscience’ test when joinder is not feasible; the pleading must name absent required persons and explain why they are not joined.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined for claims from the same transaction or series with a common question; the court may order separate trials.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Not grounds for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants exposing the plaintiff to double or multiple liability may be required to interplead; a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Rule 23(a) prerequisites and (b)(1)-(3) types track the older federal rule; certification order as soon as practicable; best practicable notice with opt-out in (b)(3) classes. Dismissal or compromise needs court approval and notice to the class. The text lacks the later federal additions (class counsel appointment, attorney-fee procedure, interlocutory appeal of certification).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"Complaint must be verified, plead contemporaneous ownership, and plead with particularity demand efforts or why none was made; dismissal or compromise needs court approval and notice.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Derivative action; unincorporated associations","s":"Actions by or against members as a class through representatives, if they fairly and adequately protect the association’s interests; Rule 23(d)-(e) procedures apply.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right (statute or impaired interest not adequately represented) and permissive intervention, on timely motion with an accompanying pleading. A party challenging the constitutionality of a state statute in a case where the state is not a party must serve the Attorney General in time to allow intervention.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"On death, any party or the successor may move to substitute, serving non-parties as for a summons. Unlike the federal rule there is no 90-day deadline after a statement of death; if no motion is made, the action is subject to dismissal under Rule 41(b). Incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers as in the federal rule.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Unlike the federal rule, there are no mandatory initial disclosures and no required discovery conference (an ESI conference happens only by agreement or court order). Scope is the older federal standard: any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter, including information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, limited for cumulative, duplicative, or unduly burdensome discovery. Expert discovery is by interrogatory (identity, subject, substance of facts and opinions, grounds), document requests for materials relied on, and deposition once the interrogatory is answered; written expert reports are not required, drafts and most attorney-expert communications are protected. Privilege logs, ESI cost-shifting, and clawback of produced privileged ESI follow the federal model. Protective-order motions need a good-faith conference certification. Duty to supplement is narrower than the federal rule (witness and expert identity, knowingly incorrect answers).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"Perpetuation of testimony before suit follows state statute; pending appeal, the Superior Court may allow depositions on motion showing who will be examined, the expected testimony, and why.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In state, before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court appointee (no officer needed for non-stenographic depositions); out of state, before an authorized person, a commissioned person, or by letter of request. Officers related to or employed by a party or counsel, or financially interested, are disqualified.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding depositions","s":"Written stipulations may change who takes, when, where, and how depositions are taken.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"No leave needed except for prisoners, repeat depositions, or a plaintiff’s deposition within 30 days after service (unless the defendant has started discovery or the witness is leaving Rhode Island). Notice must state the recording method; organizational (30(b)(6)) designations are served and filed before the deposition; remote depositions by stipulation or order. No federal-style 1-day/7-hour limit or 10-deposition cap in the text; the court may limit time by order. Non-party, non-lawyer attendees require 48 hours’ notice to all counsel. Objections concise and non-suggestive; instructions not to answer only for privilege, a court limit, or to seek a protective order. Witness review: 30 days after notice the transcript is available, if requested before the deposition ends. The original transcript is not filed; the examining attorney keeps it sealed.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Leave needed only for prisoners or repeat depositions. Cross questions within 14 days of service of the notice; redirect within 7 days after cross; recross within 7 days after redirect.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Federal-style uses, with Rhode Island’s ‘out of the state’ unavailability ground (rather than the federal 100-mile rule). A videotaped deposition of a medical witness or other non-party expert, taken by stipulation or order, may be used at trial for any purpose whether or not the witness is available. Objections to written-question form within the time for the next questions and within 5 days after the last questions.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Plaintiff needs leave to serve interrogatories within 60 days after service of process on the defendant. Answers and objections due within 40 days after service (the federal rule allows 30). Limit: 30 interrogatories in total (federal: 25), more only by court order for good cause. Objections not timely served are waived. Amended answers must be served within a reasonable time and no later than 30 days before the trial date; after that only by motion. Business-records option as in the federal rule.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","s":"May be served on the plaintiff after commencement and on any other party with or after service of process. Written response due within 40 days after service (federal: 30), but a defendant gets 60 days after service of the summons and complaint. Must state for each item whether production will be permitted or the objection and reasons; ESI form rules as in the federal rule. Non-parties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"On motion for good cause when a party’s condition (including blood group) is in controversy, by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. On request, the examining party must provide the detailed report and is then entitled to reciprocal reports; requesting the report waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Matters are deemed admitted unless a written answer or objection is served within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack-of-knowledge answers require a statement of reasonable inquiry. Admissions bind only in the pending action (and a later action between the same parties on the same claim). There is no numerical limit in the text.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel need a good-faith conference certification; expenses usually follow. A Rhode Island feature: an order compelling discovery may expressly provide for a final judgment of dismissal or default if the order is not obeyed within 30 days (or other set time), the so-called conditional order. No sanctions, absent exceptional circumstances, for ESI lost by routine good-faith operation of a system. Expenses for unjustified refusal to admit; sanctions for failing to attend one’s own deposition or to answer interrogatories or document requests at all.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"Right preserved under R.I. Const. art. I, § 15 or statute. A written jury demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue (federal: 14) and filed; others may demand additional issues within 10 days. Failure to serve and file a demand waives the jury.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise or no right exists; the court may in its discretion order a jury despite a missed demand; advisory or consent juries allowed.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial; continuances","s":"Cases are assigned by court order or rules of practice, or on motion with notice, but no motion to assign for trial may be made within 40 days after service on all necessary parties. Continuances only on motion for good cause; a motion based on an absent witness requires a detailed affidavit, and one based on illness requires a physician’s certificate.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"Voluntary dismissal by notice before service of an answer or summary-judgment motion, or by stipulation signed by all appearing parties; a second notice dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits. The court may dismiss for lack of prosecution an action pending more than 5 years, or at any time for failure to comply with the rules or to proceed when reached for trial, after notice. Involuntary dismissals (other than jurisdiction, venue, or indispensable party) are on the merits unless the order says otherwise.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Actions with a common question may be consolidated even if pending in different counties; separate trials of claims or issues may be held in the county of filing or a different county.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence","s":"Testimony is taken orally in open court; admissibility follows state statutes and the Rhode Island Rules of Evidence. Rhode Island additions: only one attorney per side may examine a given witness, and the attorney must stand unless permitted otherwise; examination is limited to direct, cross, and redirect without leave. The party with the burden of proof opens and closes; on motions, the movant. Exhibits not withdrawn within 30 days after final disposition need not be preserved.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"Official records proved by official publication or attested copy, with certification for out-of-state or foreign records; a no-record statement is admissible.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"Notice by pleading or other reasonable written notice; the court may consider any relevant source, and its determination is a ruling of law.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Issued by the clerk, a notary public, or another authorized officer; served by an authorized officer or any non-party age 18 or older, with one day’s fees and mileage tendered (not required for the state); may be served anywhere in Rhode Island. Prior notice of a document subpoena must be served on all parties. Written objections to production within 14 days after service (or before the compliance date if sooner) stop production until the court orders it. Federal-style protections against undue burden, ESI provisions, and quash grounds; disobedience may be contempt.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; making the objection and grounds known when the ruling is made preserves it, and the court may allow a standing objection to a line of testimony.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court or counsel may conduct voir dire, under oath on request; 1 or 2 alternates may be seated.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors","s":"Six jurors and a unanimous verdict unless the parties stipulate otherwise with court approval.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"Special verdicts and general verdicts with interrogatories, with federal-style rules for inconsistent answers; an omitted issue is waived unless jury submission is demanded before the jury retires. Separate verdicts on multiple counts may be required.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law in jury trials; new trial; conditional rulings","s":"Motion any time before submission, stating the judgment sought and the law and facts. At the close of the opponent’s evidence the court may instead order an involuntary dismissal without prejudice. Renewed motion within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days), with an optional joined or alternative new-trial motion; conditional new-trial rulings as in the federal rule. A timely Rule 50(b) motion stops the appeal clock under Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Argument of counsel; instructions to jury","s":"Each party gets 1 hour for closing argument (40 minutes in cases commenced in the District Court) unless more is allowed. Written requests for instructions at close of evidence or earlier; an instruction error is preserved only by objecting before the jury retires, stating the matter and grounds distinctly.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court; judgment on partial findings","s":"In non-jury trials the court finds facts specially and states conclusions of law, orally on the record or in a decision; not needed on Rule 12 or 56 motions. Motion to amend findings within 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). Judgment on partial findings, or dismissal without prejudice, after a party is fully heard.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Masters (including referees, auditors, examiners) appointed by agreement or, without agreement, only as an exception: in jury cases for accounts or complicated issues, in non-jury cases for account and damage computation or an exceptional condition. First meeting within 20 days of the order of reference. In non-jury cases findings are accepted unless clearly erroneous, and objections to the report are due within 10 days after notice of filing. In jury cases findings may be read to the jury as evidence. This follows the older federal Rule 53, not the 2003 revision.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgment; costs","s":"‘Judgment’ includes any appealable order. Rule 54(b) partial final judgment requires an express determination of no just reason for delay and an express direction. Default judgments cannot exceed or differ from the demand. Costs go to the prevailing party as provided by statute and the rules, taxed by the clerk on 10 days’ notice with an itemized bill; objections are heard by the court. Deposition costs, if objected to, only if the deposition was reasonably necessary. No federal-style Rule 54(d)(2) fee-motion deadline appears in the text.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"Clerk enters default on affidavit. Clerk enters judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant; otherwise application to the court, with 10 days’ written notice to a party who has appeared (federal: 7). A Servicemembers Civil Relief Act affidavit is required before any default judgment. Default set aside for good cause; default judgment under Rule 60(b).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move after 20 days from commencement; a defending party at any time. The motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing, and opposing affidavits may be served any time before the hearing day. This tracks the pre-2010 federal text (‘no genuine issue as to any material fact’), with no statement-of-undisputed-facts requirement in the rule. An opponent must set out specific facts by affidavit or other materials; Rule 56(f) relief if facts are unavailable; bad-faith affidavits draw expenses and possible contempt.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory relief under the statute follows these rules; jury demands per Rules 38-39; another adequate remedy does not bar relief; the court may advance the hearing.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"The clerk enters judgment on a general verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs, or denial of all relief; otherwise the court approves the form first. Every judgment must be on a separate document and is entered when set forth and signed by the clerk; entry is not delayed for costs. The clerk may enter agreed judgments for a sum certain or denying relief, except where a party is a minor or incompetent. Entry starts the 20-day appeal period under Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials; amendment of judgments","s":"New trial for error of law at trial or any ground historically recognized in Rhode Island courts. Motion for new trial, and motion to alter or amend, must be served no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28 days). Opposing affidavits within 10 days of service, extendable by up to 20 more days. The court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days of entry. Rule 6(b) bars extending these times.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical errors corrected any time (with Supreme Court leave once the appeal is docketed). Rule 60(b) grounds as in the federal rule; motions within a reasonable time and, for mistake, newly discovered evidence, or fraud, not more than 1 year after entry. The motion does not suspend the judgment; independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief preserved. A Rule 60(b) motion does not stop the appeal clock (it is not listed in Sup. Ct. Art. I, R. 4(a)).","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"No execution or enforcement until 20 days after entry or until the appeal time expires, but injunctions and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered. Stay pending appeal by supersedeas bond, effective when the court approves it; the State need not post one. Stays pending Rule 60 motions and Rule 54(b) judgments in the court’s discretion.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Inability of a judicial officer to proceed","s":"A successor judicial officer may continue after certifying familiarity with the record; in non-jury matters, must recall on request a material, disputed, available witness.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Replevin","s":"Unlike the federal rule (which defers to state seizure law), Rhode Island’s Rule 64 sets the procedure: a writ of replevin on motion, never ex parte, on a showing of probable success and substantial need for possession pending suit; the court may instead order the defendant to give security.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"Preliminary injunction only with notice; hearing may be consolidated with trial. A TRO without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint and an attorney’s written certification of notice efforts; it expires within no more than 10 days (federal: 14) unless extended for a like period or by consent. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. A TRO denied by one judge may not be re-presented to another without a material change of circumstances. Security is discretionary (‘may order’), not mandatory as in the federal rule.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"A Rhode Island-specific rule: ordinarily one receiver, a Rhode Island resident; ex parte temporary receivers only on a sworn written showing plus attorney certificate; no forum-shopping a denied ex parte application. The decree fixes deadlines for inventory, reports, and claims; a temporary receivership over 30 days requires such a decree. Receivers report every 30 days. Receiver-lawyers generally may not hire separate counsel. Fees only after hearing; the action may be dismissed only by court order.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court; registry","s":"Deposits by leave and on notice; registry funds by certified or cashier’s check to the Registry of the Superior Court for the county, withdrawn only on written court order; no clerk fees on disbursement.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment; payment into court","s":"A defending party may serve an offer of judgment more than 10 days before trial; the offeree has 10 days to accept in writing (federal: 14 days for both). If the final judgment is not more favorable, the offeree pays costs incurred after the offer. Rhode Island adds payment into court: the defendant may deposit money and plead it is the most owed; the claimant may accept with costs, reject, or accept as part payment and try damages only. Unaccepted offers are inadmissible except on interest or costs.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution; supplementary proceedings; post-judgment attachment and trustee process","s":"Execution procedure follows statute; discovery in aid of judgment allowed. A Rhode Island-specific citation in supplementary proceedings orders a debtor whose execution was returned unsatisfied to appear for examination; served at least 6 days before the appearance; failure to appear can lead to a civil body attachment. The court may enter installment-payment decrees enforceable by contempt. Post-judgment non-wage trustee process requires sending the debtor notice with an exemption hearing 5 to 9 days later. Wage attachment only on motion, with a hearing 10 to 25 days after mailing notice; the writ expires unless served on the employer within 45 days of issuance.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts","s":"If a party fails to perform a directed act, the court may appoint someone to do it, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title to in-state property directly.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","s":"Non-parties may enforce, and be bound by, orders as if parties.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"72","t":"Certification","s":"Where a statute allows certification of an action or question to the Supreme Court, a party’s motion must be served at least 10 days before the hearing and specify the matter; on the court’s own initiative the parties must be heard first. (Unlike federal Rule 72, this is not about magistrate judges.)","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"73-76","t":"[Repealed]","s":"Repealed; no current text.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"77","t":"Superior Court and clerks","s":"Court always open for filing; trials in open court; clerk’s office open business days. Docket notation of an order or judgment is sufficient notice for all purposes, though parties may also serve notice. A proposed written order filed with a certificate of service is entered by the clerk if no objection or competing order is filed within 4 days.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"78","t":"Stenographic reports; transcripts","s":"Transcript rates capped at $3.00 per page for originals and $1.50 per page for copies, with a $5.00 minimum; ordering and payment follow the Supreme Court Finance Office policy.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"79","t":"Books and records kept by the clerk","s":"The clerk keeps the civil docket, with consecutive file numbers, chronological entries, entry dates, and jury demands, plus required indices.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"80","t":"Review of administrative action","s":"Where a statute provides Superior Court review of agency action, review begins by complaint stating the grounds and relief; no responsive pleading unless required. Time limits come from the governing statute; serve the agency and all parties under Rule 5. Fact trials are non-jury unless a right exists.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability of rules","s":"The rules do not apply at the process and pleading stages of probate appeals, condemnation, mechanics’ lien enforcement, and statutory receivership or dissolution petitions, and do not apply at all to assignments for creditors, tax-sale foreclosure of redemption, naturalization, most prerogative writs, and habeas corpus. On a District Court appeal for trial de novo, the plaintiff may replead within 10 days after certification, the defendant then has 10 more days, a jury demand must be served within 10 days after certification unless made below, and Rules 26-37 discovery is unavailable without an order showing injustice or undue hardship. Mandamus follows these rules.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit Superior Court jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"Rules of practice and orders","s":"Rules of practice adopted by a majority of Superior Court judicial officers and administrative orders of the presiding justice have the force of these rules.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"Forms on the Judiciary’s website are sufficient under the rules.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"Out-of-state counsel","s":"No one who is not a Rhode Island attorney may act for a party in Superior Court without leave of the Superior or Supreme Court. Leave is by miscellaneous petition in the Supreme Court’s form, with certifications from the applicant and Rhode Island associate counsel and a client certification, subject to Sup. Ct. Art. II, Rule 9. Unless excused, a Rhode Island lawyer prepared to continue must be present in the courtroom for the entire proceeding.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"These amended rules took effect November 5, 2014, and apply to pending actions unless application would be infeasible or unjust.","u":"https://www.courts.ri.gov/Courts/superiorcourt/Documents/SuperiorCourtRulesOfCivilProcedure.pdf"}]});
